 "observations": [
  "The page number «150» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 39», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman; L04, the place of the node of Mars, «Locus autem draconis 0. 21. 54», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CN)»: no signs, 21° 54′, the value of chapter 19 of the text (p. 15, JP2 0047) and of p. 64 (JP2 0096, L38). The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the last two titles, «C 62r, O 119v,» and «N 15v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on pp. 144-149 (JP2 0176-0181), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Semita numeri (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmen-tum1) proprietatum omnium», hyphenated, and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium» in smaller type; «Sublimatio2) ascensus Martis examinata3)»; «Examinatio ipsius elhezae4)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Martis»; «Latitudo Martis5) ascendentis6)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L10), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L11, the units, in bold.",
  "L12-L41. Checked: each value of the Sublimatio is 4s 8° 24′, the apogee of Mars (text, chapter 18, pp. 14-15, JP2 0046-0047; 128° 24′), less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row, a half minute counted as a whole, in every row. The Examinatio argumenti agrees to within 0.6′ in every row with the equation of the anomaly of an epicycle, arc tan (r sin x : (60 + r cos x)), for r = 38.71, the radius that fits the thirty values best; the radius that reproduces each value is between 38.6 and 39.1 from 2° to 30°. The Latitudo secunda is 3° 45′ · sin x to within 0.5″ in every row; its greatest value, 3° 45′, is to be compared with p. 152.",
  "L12-L41, the other columns, compared from row to row: the Examinatio centri increases by 11′ and 12′ in turn to 15°, then by 10′ or 11′ a degree, to 5° 35′ at 30°, the half of 11° 10′; it lies within 2.2′ below 11° 10′ · sin x in every row. The Statio prima increases from 5s 7° 28′ by 0′ or 1′ a degree to 13°, by 1′ or 2′ to 24°, by 4′ from 24° to 25° and by 3′ a degree from 25° on, to 5s 8° 15′ at 30°. The Latitudo prima is 2° 14′ to 4°, 2° 13′ to 8°, 2° 12′ to 12°, 2° 11′ to 18°, 2° 10′ to 26° and 2° 9′ from 27° on.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 1s 0°, is C's; O has 0. Checked: the Sublimatio 4s 3° 8′ at 27° (note b, C, against O's 7) agrees with the rule; the Examinatio centri 0° 34′ at 3° (note c, C, against O's 24) with the steps of the column; the Latitudo secunda 1° 20′ 38″, 1° 38′ 38″ and 1° 45′ 38″ at 21°, 26° and 28° (notes d, C and O, where N has «xxxo»), 1° 20′ 38.0″, 1° 38′ 38.0″ and 1° 45′ 37.9″ computed.",
  "L32, the Examinatio argumenti at 21°: a small slanting mark at the foot of the line after the 8 of «8 13»; not transcribed. Italics: «om.». N01-N02: the ellipsis «. . . .» of the lemma as printed. L11, «Sig» without its point in the units of the Sublimatio, and N02, «Martis C» without its bracket: see the critical notes."
 ],
